6519845 1071 "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nWork on intervals where $x>0$ and $y>0$, and where the displayed coefficients are defined.\n\n- **I.** $y' + 4 \\cdot \\sqrt{x} \\cdot y = 3 \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot \\log{\\left(y \\right)}$\n- **II.** $y' + 2 \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot y^{2} = x \\cdot y^{3}$\n- **III.** $y' + 3 \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot y = \\frac{4 \\cdot \\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{y}}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139535586 17592186045443 true] [17592186519845 1072 #uuid "09ed8d3f-e8c4-5182-b1db-e5d98c04df75" 13194139535586 17592186045443 true] [17592186519845 1074 "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options. Work on the stated positive domain, so dividing by powers of $y$ is valid.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains a function of $y$ that is not a single power of $y$.\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\n- Equation III is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=3 \\cdot x^{2},\\quad q(x)=4 \\cdot \\sqrt{x},\\quad n=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, select III." 13194139535586 17592186045443 true] [17592186519846 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519857 13194139535586 17592186045443 true] [17592186519846 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519874 13194139535586 17592186045443 true] [17592186519846 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408166 13194139535586 17592186045443 true] [17592186519846 1077 "I" 13194139535586 17592186045443 true] [17592186519846 1078 #edb/ref 17592186519857 13194139535586 17592186045443 true] [17592186519846 1079 #uuid "0bc965c8-4776-5a91-92f3-2e53b74683f4" 13194139535586 17592186045443 true] [17592186519847 1080 "false" 13194139535586 17592186045443 true] [17592186519847 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535586 1759218604