where the displayed coefficients are defined.\n\n- **I.** $y^{2}y' - x \\cdot y^{5} = 4 \\cdot y^{4}$\n- **II.** $yy' - 5 \\cdot x \\cdot \\sqrt{y} = x^{2}$\n- **III.** $y' + 2 \\cdot y \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)} = x \\cdot \\log{\\left(y \\right)}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139535586 17592186045443 true] [17592186519860 1072 #uuid "41658dd9-b512-54b4-8eaf-1cd8fd36a4fa" 13194139535586 17592186045443 true] [17592186519860 1074 "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options. Work on the stated positive domain, so dividing by powers of $y$ is valid.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. Dividing by $y^{2}$, we get\n\n$$\ny' - x \\cdot y^{3} = 4 \\cdot y^{2}\n$$\n\n The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. Dividing by $y$, we get\n\n$$\ny' - \\frac{5 \\cdot x}{\\sqrt{y}} = \\frac{x^{2}}{y}\n$$\n\n The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains a function of $y$ that is not a single power of $y$.\n\nTherefore, select none of the given equations." 13194139535586 17592186045443 true] [17592186519861 1080 "true" 13194139535586 17592186045443 true] [17592186519861 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535586 17592186045443 true] [17592186519861 1083 #uuid "42b0e886-861f-5f24-a07d-d5ccb62ab994" 13194139535586 17592186045443 true] [17592186519862 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519847 13194139535586 17592186045443 true] [17592186519862 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519883 13194139535586 17592186045443 true] [17592186519862 1076 #edb/ref 17592186408166 13194139535586 17592186045443 true] [17592186519862 1077 "I" 13194139535586 17592186045443 true] [17592186519862 1078 #edb/ref 17592186519847 13194139535